-- title:
--   Poles of the blow-up chart along Igusa and off-orbit valuations
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $5 \le q$ and $\ell$ a prime with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \ne q$; $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`) and $\ell \nmid M'$ (`hℓM'`). $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\xi \in L$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota\xi = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, and `hK` identifies it as [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79): the subfield generated over $L$ by the image, under coefficientwise extension of scalars, of the $q$-expansion function field of the modular curve of level $N_0 = (q\ell)^2M'$ attached to the subgroup $H = \ker\bigl((\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times\bigr)$ of $(\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times$.
--
--   $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, with $L$ as fraction field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal (`hAq`) and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hξA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and has Laurent series the image of the classical $j$-series [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`). Further, $\varpi$ is a uniformiser of $A$ (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C =$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, and $\mathfrak{X} =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the scheme obtained by glueing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ to the corresponding chart for $j^{-1}$ along the middle chart, with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and the map `ιFin` from $X_{\mathrm{Fin}} = \operatorname{Spec} C$. The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal (`hy`) and contains the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` asserts that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` postulates a Drinfeld chart presentation at every such point: for every point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$, every stalk element $\varpi_z$ equal to the germ at $z$ of the global image of $\varpi$ under `toBase` and lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin` image $z$ whose ideal satisfies the same supersingularity condition as above, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with maximal ideal generated by $\sigma\varpi$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk of $\mathfrak{X}$ at $z$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/(\mathrm{C}(\sigma(\varpi_t^{q+1}))v - fu)$, subject to the following seven clauses, in which `toC` denotes the map from the stalk to its completion, `mkS` the quotient map onto $S$, and `germY` the germ homomorphism $C \to \mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X},z}$ coming from `ιFin` at $y'$: (1) $e$ carries the germ of the image of $a \in A$ to the class of $\mathrm{C}(\sigma a)$; (2) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau \in \mathrm{Aut}_L(K)$ with [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — the condition that $\tau$ acts on ratios of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_H(N_0)$ as the slash action of the conjugated matrix $\begin{pmatrix} a & b/(q\ell) \\ q\ell c & d\end{pmatrix}$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, read through any embedding $L \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$ — the automorphism $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$; (3) if in addition $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$, then the restriction of such a $\tau$ to $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'$; (4) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, such a $\tau$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ with $\theta$ compatible with $\tau$ through $e \circ$ `toC` $\circ$ `germY`, $\theta$ fixing all constants, $\theta(X_{j}) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$, $c \equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (5) for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1, P_2$ of $S$, each omitting the class of $X_0$ or of $X_1$, each containing the class of $\mathrm{C}(\sigma\varpi)$, and each containing an element $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ then $P_1$ and $P_2$ have distinct contractions to the stalk along $e \circ$ `toC`; (6) for a prime $P$ of $S$ omitting the class of $X_0$ or of $X_1$, containing the class of $\mathrm{C}(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing some $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ satisfies `toC (germY a)` $\in e^{-1}P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (7) the element [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) — the $j$-series with exponents scaled by $q\ell$ — lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with the difference of the two lying in $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_{i \le e_0} \mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b \in W$ not both in the maximal ideal with $a^qb-ab^q$ in the maximal ideal, such that $e$ carries `toC (germY ·)` of that difference to the class of $h$.
--
--   Fixed data are then imposed: a point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$ with $\varpi_z$, `hϖz`, `hz` as above, a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin` image $z$ (`hy'`) satisfying the supersingularity condition `hss'`, and `hy'y` asserting that the ideal of $y'$ is $y$; moreover one chart presentation $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1$ with $u_1, v_1$ units (`hu₁`, `hv₁`), $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), $\sigma_1\varpi$ generating the maximal ideal of $W_1$ (`hσ₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the completed stalk with $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\mathrm{C}(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$, for which the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts the seven clauses (1)–(7) just listed.
--
--   The ideal $J \subseteq C$ is pinned by `hJ` as the infimum of the set of ideals of the form $\tau^{-1}$ of the contraction along $e_1 \circ$ `toC` $\circ$ `germY` of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $\mathrm{C}(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, the infimum being taken over $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and automorphisms $\tau$ of $K$ over $L$ that are level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ in the above sense and preserve $C$. The $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$ is pinned by `hB` as the algebra generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K : x\varpi_t \in J\}$, that is $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$. Finally $W$ is a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`), and three groups of hypotheses are imposed: `hR1` states that $C \subseteq B$ and that every element of $K$ is a ratio of elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2` states that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) states that $W$ meets $L$ exactly in the image of $A$, that the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that an element of $C$ lies in $y$ precisely when it lies in the maximal ideal of $W$, and that $f \in K$ lies in $W$ precisely when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h$ a unit of $W$.
--
--   The conclusion is a conjunction of two statements.
--
--   First, there exists $b \in B$ such that for every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ whose intersection with $L$ is the image of $A$ and in which the image of $\varpi$, if it belongs to $V$, belongs to the maximal ideal of $V$, the following holds: if $j \in V$ and every element of $V$ of the form $p(j)$ with $p$ a monic polynomial over $A$ is a unit of $V$, then $b \notin V$. Thus $b$ has a pole along every valuation of Igusa type, at which $j$ is residually transcendental.
--
--   Second, for every maximal ideal $y''$ of $C$ containing the image of $\varpi$ which is not matched with $y$ by any relevant automorphism — that is, such that for no $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and no level automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ does one have, for all $b \in C$ with $\tau(b) \in C$, the equivalence $b \in y \iff \tau(b) \in y''$ — there exists $b \in B$ with the following property: for every $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, every level automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, and every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ which contains $C$ and in which every element of $C$ whose $\tau^{-1}$-image lies in $C$ and in $y''$ lies in the maximal ideal of $V$, one has $b \notin V$. Thus a single element of $B$ has a pole along every valuation centred on a translate of $y''$.
--
--   This is one of the two pole clauses for the blow-up chart $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$ of the $j$-finite chart algebra at a supersingular maximal ideal $y$: it produces elements of $B$ with poles along all Igusa-type valuations and along all valuations centred on translates of a supersingular maximal ideal outside the $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$-orbit of $y$. It feeds the assembly of the supersingular affine chart at auxiliary level, where such poles are what separates the chart from the other components and ends of the integral model.
